#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073464 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073806 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d8a8280-5248-57e4-a5ed-14e281f3db77" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Square Root Expressions"}] :question/id #uuid "cf793c45-6c89-50c8-bb28-d066d2b8342a" :question/math-academy-id "e-1370" :question/problem "Simplify $8\\sqrt 3 - 5\\sqrt 3 + \\sqrt 5$." :question/worked-solution "We can combine the two $\\sqrt{3}$ terms together, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8\\sqrt{3} - 5\\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{5} &=  \\\\\n(8 - 5)\\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{5} &=  \\\\\n&= 3\\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073663 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a31c2b6-585a-5715-98f8-4b82605f4c22" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions Containing Cube Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "e5202e06-b6a8-5d74-a395-fe7c56b8e9bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-9728" :question/problem "Simplify $2\\sqrt[3]{5} +5 \\sqrt{10} -3 \\sqrt[3]{40}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we use the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt[3]{40} &= \\sqrt[3]{8 \\cdot 5} = \\sqrt[3]{8} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{5} = 2\\sqrt[3]{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\sqrt[3]{5} + 5\\sqrt{10} - 3\\sqrt[3]{40} &=  \\\\\n2\\sqrt[3]{5} + 5\\sqrt{10} - 3(2\\sqrt[3]{5}) &=  \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt[3]{5} + 5\\sqrt{10} - 6\\sqrt[3]{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can combine the two $\\sqrt[3]{5}$ terms together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\sqrt[3]{5} + 5\\sqrt{10} - 6\\sqrt[3]{5} &=  \\\\\n5\\sqrt{10} + (2\\sqrt[3]{5} - 6\\sqrt[3]{5}) &=  \\\\\n5\\sqrt{10} + (2 - 6)\\sqrt[3]{5} &=  \\\\\n&= 5\\sqrt{10} - 4\\sqrt[3]{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073664 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "622cd109-3764-5f32-8205-046be4b57c54" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Square Root Expressions Using the Product Rule for Radicals"}] :question/id #uuid "c1f1d037-45a6-5e72-a735-0c0512290120" :question/math-academy-id "e-9729" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $3\\sqrt{80} -8\\sqrt{5}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we use the product rule for radicals to simplify $\\sqrt{80}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{80} &= \\sqrt{16 \\cdot 5} = \\sqrt{16} \\cdot \\sqrt{5} = 4\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\sqrt{80} - 8\\sqrt{5} &=  \\\\\n3(4\\sqrt{5}) - 8\\sqrt{5} &=  \\\\\n&= 12\\sqrt{5} - 8\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can combine the two $\\sqrt{5}$ terms together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12\\sqrt{5} - 8\\sqrt{5} &=  \\\\\n(12 - 8)\\sqrt{5} &=  \\\\\n&= 4\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
